{
  "moduleId": "m00119",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00119:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Organometallic Coupling Reactions"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "808ac7df-6b01-4eb0-8904-df67a4dab406"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00119:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "1-bromobutane reacts with 2 equivalents of lithium in pentane to form butyllithium and LiBr. C1 of butyllithium (ball-and-stick model in an electrostatic potential map) is labeled basic and nucleophilic."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00119:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "The reaction of 2 equivalents of methyllithium with copper iodide in ether forms lithium dimethylcopper (a Gilman reagent) and lithium iodide."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "Lithium dimethylcopper and 1-iododecane in ether at 0 degrees Celcius forms undecane (90%), lithium iodide, and methyl copper."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00119:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "Trans-1-iodo-1-nonene reacts with lithium dibutylcopper to form trans-5-tridecene (71%), n-butylcopper, and LiI. Iodobenzene reacts with lithium dimethylcopper to form toluene (91%), methyl copper, and LiI."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00119:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "The reaction of cis-1-bromo-9-octadecene with lithium dipentylcopper form muscalure (9Z-tricosene)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00119:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00006",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00006-alt",
          "text": "RX reacts with lithium di-R' copper to form an intermediate which further reacts to form R-R' and R'-Cu."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_006.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00119:para:para-00006"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00007",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00007-alt",
          "text": "The reaction of an aromatic substituted boronic acid and iodobenzene in the presence of palladium-tetrakis(triphenylphosphine), calcium carbonate, and tetrahydrofuran form a biaryl compound (92%)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_007.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00007",
      "segmentId": "m00119:para:para-00007"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00008",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00008-alt",
          "text": "The reaction of para-methylbenzeneboronic acid and ortho-chloro-benzonitrile in the presence of palladium catalyst and potassium carbonate forms an intermediate that further produces Valsartan (Diovan)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_008.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00008",
      "segmentId": "m00119:para:para-00008"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00009",
      "class": "scaled-down",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00119:caption:fig-00009-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00119:alt:fig-00009-alt",
          "text": "Via a three-step cyclic reaction, an aromatic halide reacts with catalyst, forming an organopalladium intermediate, which reacts with aromatic boronic acid, yielding diarylpalladium intermediate. Decomposition produces a biaryl product."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_07_009.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
